<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fatimid Caliphate</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Fatimid, Fatimids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> Shi'a caliphate founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> in 909 that conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 969 and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. At its height it ruled North Africa, Egypt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> and rivalled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>. It was weakened by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusades</a> and was abolished by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> in 1171, opening the way to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate.jpg" alt="Fatimid Caliphate" width="320" height="183" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nourerrahmane (using file uploaded by Omar-toons as template) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Fatimid_Caliphate.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Fatimids led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Isma'ilism</a>, a revolutionary Shi'a movement that was at once political and religious, philosophical and social, and that originally proclaimed the arrival of an Islamic messiah. They traced their descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatima">Fatima</a>, the daughter of Muhammad, and her husband Ali, and called themselves simply the Alid dynasty. The origins of the movement are obscure before the late ninth century. Its founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallah-al-mahdi-billah">Abdallah al-Mahdi Billah</a>, was Arab in origin, but its first army was made up of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutama-berbers">Kutama Berbers</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-kabylia">Little Kabylia</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> was then held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabids</a>, an Arab dynasty that ruled nominally for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>. In 893 the missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-abdallah-al-shii">Abu Abdallah al-Shi'i</a> settled among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutama-berbers">Kutama</a>, and over two years he won most of the tribes by persuasion or coercion and set up an Isma'ili theocratic state at Tazrut, near Mila. Between 902 and 909 his forces conquered Aghlabid Ifriqiya, and al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> was accepted as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> and first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> in 909.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>For much of the tenth century the Fatimids rivalled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> for control of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>. Their capitals were first al-Mahdiyya, on a peninsula on the coast of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, then al-Mansuriyya near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a>. In 969 the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawhar-al-siqilli">Jawhar</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, which the Ikhshidids held, and founded al-Qahira, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, several miles north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>'s centre moved there in 973, and Egypt became the political, cultural and religious centre of the empire.</p><p>After the reigns of al-'Aziz and al-Hakim, the long reign of al-Mustansir left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> aloof from affairs and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a> more powerful. Factionalism in the army led to a civil war in the 1060s, followed by a revival under the vizier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badr-al-jamali">Badr al-Jamali</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> pressed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in the 1070s and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> arrived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> in 1097. In 1171 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> abolished the dynasty and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid dynasty</a>. After al-Mustansir his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismailism">Nizar</a> and al-Musta'li both claimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a>, splitting the Isma'ilis into Nizari and Musta'li branches.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Fatimid society was highly pluralistic. Isma'ili <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiism">Shi'ism</a> was the religion of the state and the court, but most Muslims stayed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a>, a large part of the population remained Christian, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were a smaller minority. Non-Muslims were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">dhimmis</a> and paid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a>, and scholars generally agree that Fatimid rule was tolerant and inclusive. Its leaders made little headway in persuading the population to adopt their beliefs.</p><p>The army began with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutama-berbers">Kutama Berbers</a>. From the reign of al-'Aziz Turkish freedmen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylamites">Daylamis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> and Black infantry bought as slaves in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> joined it, and the Kutama lost their privileged place. Fatimid art is known for lively figurative motifs, angular floriated Kufic script, lustre ceramics and carved rock crystal. The mosque of al-Azhar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> still stands.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fatimid Caliphate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…also fixed Ifriqiya's religious politics: their Mu'tazilite and Hanafi establishment, set against a Maliki majority, and their reliance on Zanj slave soldiers against the Arab jund, shaped the province the <b>Fatimids</b> inherited in 909, and the conquest of Sicily they began outlived them by more than a century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>This ended the Shi'a <b>Fatimid Caliphate</b> and returned Egypt to Sunni allegiance; the Ayyubid state dates from this moment.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>To check the <b>Fatimids</b>, Abd al-Rahman occupied Melilla in 927 and Ceuta in 931 and built a network of client Berber chiefs in northern Morocco, giving the caliphate a foothold in North Africa for most of its existence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>At the same time the <b>Fatimids</b>, who had proclaimed a rival caliphate in North Africa in 909, threatened al-Andalus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sunni Aghlabids ruled until 909, when the Shiite <b>Fatimids</b> replaced them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-sicily">Emirate of Sicily · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 10th century the Idrisids became the quarry of Zenata armies fighting for the <b>Fatimids</b> and the Umayyads of Córdoba: the Miknasa under Masala ibn Habus took Fez in 917, Musa ibn Abul 'Afiya besieged the family at the fortress of Hajar an-Nasr, and after breaking the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty · Military</a></li></ul></section>
